<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/advanced/human-influences-on-ecosystems">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the advanced Biology lesson that audits our species' account with the biosphere.
    It assumes the foundational tier's <em>Ecology: populations and communities</em>,
    <em>Photosynthesis and respiration</em> and <em>Evolution by natural selection</em>, plus
    the intermediate tier's <em>Genetics: inheritance patterns</em> and <em>Plant biology</em>.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how each engine of the food-supply revolution works and name its ecological bill;</li>
    <li>use the species–area relationship to predict species loss from habitat shrinkage;</li>
    <li>trace the eutrophication chain from fertiliser to fish kill;</li>
    <li>compute a maximum sustainable yield from r and K;</li>
    <li>evaluate whether a conservation measure plausibly bends the extinction curve.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea: one species remakes the biosphere</h2>
  <p>
    No organism has ever rearranged the planet the way <em>Homo sapiens</em> has —
    converting forests to fields, doubling the nitrogen cycle, moving species between
    continents. Unit 20 of the 0610 syllabus is the accounting exercise for that
    transformation: how we boosted <strong>food supply</strong> (20.1), what
    <strong>habitat destruction</strong> costs (20.2), which <strong>pollutants</strong>
    we add (20.3), and how <strong>conservation</strong> tries to keep what remains
    (20.4). Every section pairs a benefit with a bill; the advanced-level skill is
    explaining the bill in biology — and where possible, quantifying it. The scale
    marker to keep in view throughout: humans now appropriate roughly a quarter to a
    third of the land's net primary production (the energy base every ecosystem runs
    on), and the WWF Living Planet Index — a basket of monitored vertebrate
    populations — fell about 69% between 1970 and 2018. Whatever your prior, those are
    measured accounting entries, not adjectives.
  </p>

  <h2>Feeding eight billion (20.1)</h2>
  <p>
    Food production rose because we engineered every link: <strong>agricultural
    machinery</strong> to farm larger areas efficiently, <strong>chemical
    fertilisers</strong> to replace <em>nitrate</em> and other minerals,
    <strong>insecticides</strong> and <strong>herbicides</strong> to cut the losses
    pests and weeds take, and <strong>selective breeding</strong> to raise yields of
    crops and livestock (Core list, all five). The centrepiece was the <strong>Green
    Revolution</strong> (1940s–70s): semi-dwarf, fertiliser-responsive wheat and rice
    varieties — Norman Borlaug's programme is credited with averting forecast famines —
    under which global cereal yields roughly tripled between 1960 and 2000 while the
    farmed area grew far less. The engine of the yield revolution is partly the Haber–
    Bosch process: industrial nitrogen fixation now adds ~150 million tonnes of
    reactive nitrogen per year, roughly matching all natural terrestrial fixation —
    humanity has, in the accounting sense, <em>doubled</em> the nitrogen cycle, a
    bigger perturbation of a global cycle than the carbon one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The costs show up in two systems the syllabus asks you to weigh:
    <strong>monocultures</strong> — vast single-crop fields — are efficient to harvest
    but strip the soil of specific minerals, encourage pest build-up and reduce
    biodiversity; <strong>intensive livestock</strong> production maximises output per
    animal but raises welfare, disease-spread and waste-pollution concerns. The
    advanced additions are efficiency and trophic arithmetic. Livestock convert feed to
    meat at perhaps a tenth efficiency in energy terms (cattle: ~10 kg feed per kg of
    live-weight gain), so a carnivorous diet multiplies the land, water and fertiliser
    footprint of every calorie — meat uses roughly 80% of global agricultural land
    while supplying under 20% of calories. And the "eat lower on the chain" logic is
    just the 10% trophic-efficiency rule from the food-web lesson applied to dinner.
    The open trade-off — yields versus externalities — is what precision agriculture
    (the technology chapter) is engineered around.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "meat and plants are interchangeable foods, so diet
  hardly matters for land use." It seems reasonable — both are just food. But the
  trophic arithmetic charges roughly 10 kg of feed per kilogram of cattle gain: every meat
  calorie carries ten plant calories' worth of land and fertiliser. Hence the 80%/20%
  split.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the food-supply revolution bought calories with machines,
    fertiliser and monoculture; the bill arrives as pollution and habitat conversion.
    Next: species.
  </p>

  <h2>Bulldozing habitats (20.2)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biodiversity</strong> is the number of different species in an area — and it
    falls when habitat goes: land cleared for housing, crops and livestock, resources
    extracted, fresh and marine waters polluted (Core reasons). <strong>Deforestation</strong>
    is the case study to explain (Supplement): removing forest <strong>reduces
    biodiversity and causes extinctions</strong> (specialists lose their only habitat),
    <strong>exposes soil</strong> that then erodes, <strong>increases flooding</strong>
    (no canopy to slow rainfall, no roots to hold water), and <strong>raises
    atmospheric CO₂</strong> — the trees are no longer photosynthesising, and often
    they are burned. Every food web those trees supported simplifies from the bottom
    up. The quantitative frame is the <strong>species–area relationship</strong>,
    S = c·A<sup>z</sup>, with the exponent z near 0.25 for islands and habitat
    fragments: shrink an area tenfold and it retains about 10^−0.25 ≈ 56% of its
    species — a 44% loss. That curve is why fragmentation matters as much as acreage:
    a forest cut into patches carries a long <strong>extinction debt</strong>, losing
    species for decades after the chainsaws stop, and why corridors (the conservation
    section) are a mathematical necessity, not a nicety.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tropical deforestation dominates the biodiversity bill for a structural reason:
    tropical forest holds over half of terrestrial species on ~10% of land area, so a
    hectare cleared there removes more unique evolutionary history (the phylogenetics
    lesson's EDGE logic) than a hectare anywhere else. The latest accounting: about
    10 million hectares of forest lost per year through the 2010s (FAO), with
    agricultural conversion — pasture and commodity crops like soy and palm oil — the
    leading driver, which connects this section back to 20.1: the bill for food supply
    is largely paid in habitat.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a forest cut into fragments loses only the species on
  the cleared land." The acreage view looks right. But the species–area relationship is
  nonlinear: shrink the habitat tenfold and roughly 44% of its species are lost, and the
  patches keep paying the extinction debt for decades. Arrangement matters as much as area
  — hence corridors.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> feeding eight billion converts habitat, and the species–area
    curve converts hectares into extinctions, with decades of debt to pay. Next, the
    pollutants.
  </p>

  <h2>Pollution: the oxygen sag (20.3)</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest cause-and-effect chain in ecology is <strong>eutrophication</strong>
    (Supplement): <strong>untreated sewage or excess fertiliser</strong> washes
    <strong>nitrate</strong> and other ions into water → <strong>producers</strong>
    (algae) grow explosively → when they die, <strong>decomposers</strong> multiply,
    using <strong>aerobic respiration</strong> → <strong>dissolved oxygen falls</strong> →
    <strong>fish and other oxygen-demanders die</strong>. The result downstream is the
    classic <strong>oxygen sag curve</strong>: oxygen crashes after the outfall, then
    recovers as the organic load is consumed and re-aeration wins back:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dissolved oxygen against distance from a sewage outfall — the deeper the load, the deeper the sag (saturation ≈9 mg/L in cool water)" widget="plotter" config={sagCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the plotter has one control — the pollution load. Slide
    it down and the sag shallows and shortens; slide it up and fish-stress conditions stretch
    kilometres downstream. The minimum sits below the outfall: decomposers need time
    to strip the load first.
  </p>

  <p>
    Drag the load slider and watch the mechanism: the sag's <em>depth</em> scales with
    the biochemical oxygen demand, its <em>length</em> with the river's re-aeration rate
    (fast, shallow, stony streams recover in kilometres; slow deep rivers can carry the
    sag for tens). Below ~5 mg/L of dissolved oxygen, many fish species are stressed;
    below ~2, most die — the widget's red line. Monitoring teams read exactly this
    signature: BOD upstream and down, indicator species (mayfly larvae need clean
    water; tubifex worms tolerate the worst), and the recovery distance gives the
    treatment plant its required capacity. In the sea the same nutrient subsidy makes
    coastal <strong>dead zones</strong> — the Baltic and the Gulf of Mexico's
    Mississippi-driven zone (seasonally larger than some small countries) — and the
    nitrogen surplus does double damage via the greenhouse gas nitrous oxide, ~300×
    the warming power of CO₂ per molecule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two more pollutants complete 20.3. <strong>Non-biodegradable plastics</strong>
    persist in aquatic and terrestrial habitats: they fragment into microplastics, are
    ingested by animals, smother soil and — floating in ocean gyres — entangle and
    choke marine life, all while never rotting away. <strong>Methane and carbon
    dioxide</strong> are greenhouse gases: added faster than the carbon cycle absorbs
    them, they strengthen the <strong>enhanced greenhouse effect</strong> and drive
    <strong>climate change</strong>, shifting rainfall, melting ice and pushing species
    that cannot move fast enough toward extinction. The canonical proof that pollution
    treaties can work is the timeline's 1985–87 pair: measurements found the Antarctic
    ozone hole, chemistry identified CFCs as the cause, and the Montreal Protocol
    phased the chemicals out — the ozone layer is now measurably healing, the one
    global atmospheric problem solved end to end (the environmental-science chapter
    turns it into a policy template).
  </p>

  <h2>Conservation: using without using up (20.4)</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>sustainable resource</strong> is one produced as fast as it is removed, so
    it never runs out — the definition at the heart of Core point 1. Species become
    <strong>endangered or extinct</strong> through climate change, habitat destruction,
    hunting, overharvesting, pollution and <strong>introduced species</strong> that
    outcompete natives (Core point 3) — invasives being, by most assessments, the
    second-largest extinction driver after habitat loss (think rats on islands against
    flightless birds, or the chestnut blight against American forests). The remedies
    are practical: monitoring and protecting species and habitats, education,
    <strong>captive breeding programmes</strong>, and <strong>seed banks</strong>
    (Core point 4) — with Supplement students adding artificial insemination and IVF to
    captive breeding, and the genetic warning that small populations lose variation,
    making them fragile against disease and change (the population-genetics lesson
    supplies the arithmetic: effective population sizes near 50 to dodge inbreeding
    depression, near 500 to hold adaptive variation). The policy milestones form a real
    timeline:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="sixty years of environmental policy — scrub through it (all dates verifiable)" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> scrub across the sixty years and watch the tags. The
    pollution instruments follow a documented disaster closely (ozone hole 1985, protocol
    1987); the biodiversity instruments (CITES, Rio, Kunming–Montreal) arrive without one,
    and later. A decisive measurement buys a fast treaty.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fish stocks show the arithmetic best. A population growing logistically gains
    biomass fastest at <strong>half its carrying capacity</strong> — so the smartest
    fishery keeps the stock near K/2 and harvests exactly that growth, forever. Harvest
    more than the growth and the stock shrinks; keep at it and the fishery collapses,
    which is what quotas, closed seasons, protected areas, net mesh sizes and
    monitoring (Supplement, point 6) are all designed to prevent. Play with the model:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="logistic growth — the catch you can take forever equals the growth you leave alive" widget="sliderLab" config={yieldCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> three dials: growth rate r, carrying capacity K, stock
    as a fraction of K. Hold r and K and slide the fraction: the growth readout peaks at
    0.5, where the catch equals rK/4. Either side of half, the catch falls.
  </p>

  <p>
    Work the numbers once: K = 20,000 tonnes, r = 0.6/yr gives a maximum sustainable
    yield of rK/4 = 3,000 tonnes/yr, taken from a stock of 10,000 tonnes. Now push the
    fleet harder — 3,300 tonnes/yr looks like a 10% improvement, but it exceeds
    recruitment at K/2, so the stock slides below the optimum and the sustainable
    catch falls with it: overcapacity is self-defeating arithmetic, not just ecology.
    Reality is harsher than the model — the Grand Banks cod collapse (Canadian stocks
    from ~800,000 tonnes to commercial extinction in the early 1990s, a moratorium
    still partly in force thirty years on) happened <em>with</em> quotas, because
    misreported catches, bycatch and changing ocean conditions made the "K" move under
    the managers' feet. The professional lesson: MSY is a ceiling, not a target, and
    modern fisheries policy aims below it with error margins — and the per-capita wild
    fish catch has been flat since the late 1980s while aquaculture supplies the
    growth.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a fishery harvested at exactly the maximum sustainable
  yield is perfectly managed." The model supports taking exactly the growth — but only at
  exactly K/2, every year, with a stable K. The Grand Banks collapse happened with quotas:
  misreporting, bycatch and shifting conditions moved K. MSY is a ceiling, not a target.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> conservation's remedies — reserves, captive breeding, seed
    banks, quotas — are practical and partly proven. Last question: does it work?
  </p>

  <h2>Why bother at all — and does it work?</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus ends with the reasons (Supplement, point 7):
    <strong>maintaining biodiversity</strong>, <strong>reducing extinction</strong>,
    <strong>protecting vulnerable ecosystems</strong>, and keeping ecosystem functions
    we depend on — <strong>nutrient cycling</strong> and <strong>resource
    provision</strong>: food, drugs (many first found in wild plants and fungi), fuel,
    and genes we have not yet needed. Extinction forecloses options permanently;
    conservation is the refusal to sign that receipt. The evidence that effort works is
    now quantified: a UN-sponsored analysis (Bolam et al., 2021) concluded that
    conservation action saved at least 21–32 bird and 7–16 mammal species from
    extinction between 1993 and 2020 — without it, the decade's extinction rates would
    have been roughly two to four times higher;
    humpback whales off Australia recovered from a few hundred to
    tens of thousands after the whaling moratorium; and the ozone case above shows the
    full pipeline from measurement to treaty to recovery. The open question is scale:
    the "30 by 30" target asks whether protection can outpace conversion, and the
    debate over <em>where</em> to protect (biodiversity hotspots versus large intact
    wilderness — the SLOSS question: single large or several small reserves) is
    species–area mathematics dressed as geography. The chapter's through-line: every
    influence in this unit is a transfer — biomass, nitrogen, habitat, extinction risk —
    from the biosphere's account to ours, and the advanced skill is reading the
    statements, auditing them, and knowing where the leverage is. Leverage exists at
    every stage the unit lists: demand (diet, waste — roughly a third of food produced
    is lost or wasted, the cheapest "extra land" on Earth), production (the precision
    and crop chapters' tools), and protection (the treaties, reserves and genetics of
    this chapter). Sustainability is not one discipline but the audit across all three.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Trace the chain by which fertiliser runoff ends in dead fish.</li>
    <li>A fragment retains 10% of its area. Using z ≈ 0.25, what fraction of its species remain?</li>
    <li>Why does harvesting exactly the MSY still risk collapse? Give two reasons.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) excess nitrate → algal bloom → decomposer respiration →
    oxygen crash → fish die; the sag curve plots the chain. (2) 10^−0.25 ≈
    0.56: about 56% remain — a 44% loss from 90% area loss. (3) MSY assumes the stock sits
    exactly at K/2 with a stable K; misreporting, bycatch and moving conditions break that
    (the Grand Banks), and overshoot shrinks the catch.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Food supply, habitat, pollution and conservation are one accounting problem.</li>
    <li>Industrial nitrogen fixation has roughly doubled the nitrogen cycle.</li>
    <li>Livestock convert feed at about a tenth efficiency — the 10% rule at dinner.</li>
    <li>Species–area mathematics converts habitat loss into predictable extinction, plus a long debt.</li>
    <li>Eutrophication: nutrients → bloom → decomposer respiration → oxygen crash.</li>
    <li>MSY is a ceiling at K/2, not a target; modern policy aims below it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Three advanced siblings extend this audit: <em>Population genetics</em>,
    <em>Speciation and phylogenetics</em> and <em>Molecular biology techniques</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Natural fixation and industrial Haber–Bosch each add roughly 150 million tonnes of
    reactive nitrogen per year, so the biosphere's nitrogen budget has about doubled — a
    larger perturbation than the carbon cycle's. Three routes carry the damage from one
    input: the oxygen sag in fresh water, coastal dead zones in the sea (the Baltic, the
    Gulf of Mexico's Mississippi-driven zone), and nitrous oxide — about 300 times as potent
    as CO₂ per molecule — from overloaded soils. One fertiliser decision, three bills: which
    is why this unit treats food supply and pollution as one accounting system.
  </p>
  <p>
    The frontier question is whether protection can outpace conversion. The "30 by 30"
    target sounds like simple scaling, but the SLOSS debate (single large or several small)
    is species–area mathematics dressed as geography: the same area protects different
    species sets depending on arrangement, and the extinction debt means today's reserves
    already pay for yesterday's fragmentation. The encouraging part: conservation saved at
    least 21–32 bird and 7–16 mammal species between 1993 and 2020 (Bolam et al.), and the
    Montreal Protocol ran the full pipeline from measurement to treaty to healing. What
    remains open is scale: humpback whales off Australia recovered from a few hundred to
    tens of thousands, but no degraded ecosystem has yet been handed back whole.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 20; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 47 (Conservation Biology and Biodiversity); WWF Living Planet Report 2022; FAO Global Forest Resources Assessment; UN treaties and milestones (CITES 1973; Montreal Protocol 1987; CBD 1992; Paris Agreement 2015; Kunming–Montreal GBF 2022).</p>
</TopicPage>
